<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hulü Guang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">515–572</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hulü <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-people">Guang</a> was one of the leading generals of the Northern Qi dynasty in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, repeatedly defeating the rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-zhou">Northern Zhou</a>. The son of the general Hulü Jin, he became chancellor, and his daughter married the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-wei">Gao Wei</a>. Distrusted at court and slandered with the help of Northern Zhou propaganda, he was executed in 572; Northern Zhou conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-qi">Northern Qi</a> five years later.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hulu-guang.jpg" alt="Hulü Guang" width="320" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%96%9B%E5%BE%8B%E5%85%89%EF%BC%88%E5%8C%97%E5%8F%B2%E6%BC%94%E7%BE%A9%EF%BC%89.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hulü <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-people">Guang</a>, courtesy name Mingyue, was an ethnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiele">Tiele</a>, probably the eldest son of Hulü Jin, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> chieftain who helped the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-huan">Gao Huan</a> take control of the Northern Wei state and was treated by him almost as an equal. Known in youth for his horsemanship and archery, Hulü Guang once shot the secretary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-wei">Western Wei</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-tai">Yuwen Tai</a>, Mozhe Hui, during a campaign, leading to his capture, and Gao Huan made him a general. In 547 he was created Viscount of Yongle and served against the rebel general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hou-jing">Hou Jing</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wenxuan-of-northern-qi">Emperor Wenxuan of Northern Qi</a> he fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumo-xi">Kumo Xi</a> in 552 and as a border governor took a number of minor cities from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-zhou">Northern Zhou</a>. In 560 he became governor of Bing Province and Duke of Julu. His first daughter married the crown prince Gao Bainian, who was killed by Emperor Wucheng in 564; she refused food and died a month later. In 564 he held off the southern prong of a Northern Zhou and Tujue attack and, with Duan Shao and Gao Changgong, the Prince of Lanling, relieved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>. His second daughter became empress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-wei">Gao Wei</a>.</p><p>On his father's death in 567 he inherited the title of Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianyang">Xianyang</a>. In 570 and 571 he defeated Yuwen Xian at Yiyang and built forts north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fen-river">Fen River</a>. When the prince Gao Yan killed the powerful official He Shikai in 571, Hulü stood by the emperor and broke up Gao Yan's troops. The historian Li Yanshou describes him as frugal, disliking music and beautiful women, refusing gifts, sharing his soldiers' hardships and never losing a battle from his youth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He made enemies of the officials Zu Ting, whom he scorned as a blind man in power, and Mu Tipo, whose request to marry his daughter by a concubine he had refused. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-zhou">Northern Zhou</a> general Wei Xiaokuan spread songs in couplets near Yecheng playing on Hulü's names, hinting that he would rise. Zu added lines of his own, and a subordinate, Feng Shirang, claimed that Hulü had plotted a coup when he brought his army near the capital in 571.</p><p>On 22 August 572 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-wei">Gao Wei</a> had his guard commander Liu Taozhi strangle him in the palace. Almost all his clan was killed, sparing only a young grandson, Hulü Zhong. Emperor Wu of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-zhou">Northern Zhou</a> declared a general pardon at the news, and on entering Yecheng later said that he could not have reached it had Hulü lived.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hulü Guang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the last effective ruler, Gao Wei, misrule and the execution of able generals such as <b>Hulü Guang</b> weakened the army.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-qi">Northern Qi</a></li></ul></section>
